\f3 \cb3 Method Parity\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f0\fs20 \cf20 \cb7 Major
\fs24 \cf8 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \u8232 \cb3 The prompt names IRLS and Pearson correlation but requires numeric values to match at JSON identity with no rounding or tolerance. Independent conventional IRLS and numpy correlation calculations differ from reference values only at machine precision and would nevertheless fail. Thus correct implementations are graded wrong for implementation-level arithmetic differences; narrow numeric tolerances are required.\cf5 \cb1 \
\ls8\ilvl0\cb6 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f3 \cb3 Tolerance Admits Implementation Variation\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f0\fs20 \cf20 \cb7 Major
\fs24 \cf8 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \u8232 \cb3 The task\'92s evaluation contract has a zero-width numeric band, not a tolerance suitable for implementation-level variation. A reproducible pairwise-complete Pearson calculation gives two different last-bit values from the stored reference under standard algebraically equivalent formulas, so a correct analyst is graded wrong solely because of floating-point evaluation order. The correlation\'92s Fisher-z SE is about 0.0431 (and TP53 PR\'92s delta-method SE is about 0.181), making the zero band materially tighter than the numerical and statistical noise scale.\cf5 \cb1 \
